Security incident report

Section 1: Identify the network protocol involved in the incident
The network protocol involved in the incident is HTTP (hyper text transfer protocol): A link in the original website leads to a different domain with a different IP and address. The brute force attacker used the link to lead clients to malware contained in the new website.

Section 2: Document the incident
A few hours after the attack customers emailed yummyrecipes helpdesk. Complaining that the company’s website prompted them to download a file to access free recipes.
The customers claim that after running the file, the address of the website changed  and that their devices run more slowly.
In response to this issue, the website owner tried to log in to the admin panel but failed, so he reached out to the website hosting provider.

The security analyst replicated the incident using a sandbox environment, loaded the website, was prompted to press a link for free recipes and was led to a new URL.

The security analyst reached the original website “yummyrecipesforme.com”, 
Time: 14:18:32.192571 There is an IP request from his computer through port 52444 to Google DNS server,
for the Address (A?) of the domain. (Normal connection)
At 14:18:32.204388, google DNS server answers to my machine IP is - 203.0.113.22
14:18:36.786501 to 14:18:36.786595:
there’s about 5 logs of communication between my machine using port 36086 to “yummyrecipesforme.com.https” which means destination port 80 (http).
Showing flags S, S., some ACKs (Normal connection HTTPS protocol)

And then P. (push ack) showing “HTTP: GET / HTTP/1.1”
Which is the link leading to the malware… (“free recipes”)

Because right after, at 14:20:32.192571,
my machine is sending a request to google DNS server for a domain named “greatrecipesforme.com”, and google respond has a different IP address “192.0.2.17”
The port number from my machine changed again when communication continued between my machine and the new URL On port 80 (greatrecipesforme.com.https)
which is the suspicious domain.

The attacker used the original website and HTTP initial connection to lead clients to a malware “free recipes prompt” that led to a different IP address and website URL.

A senior analyst confirmed there was a change in Javascript code that was used in the website and the downloaded file compromised users and led to the fake website.

The team reports the attacker used a brute force attack to access the account and change the admin password of the web server.
As a result of admin current default password, and no other security controls in place.

Section 3: Recommend one remediation for brute force attacks
The cybersecurity team recommends Implementing MFA for Admins and users, change passwords,
and do not use default passwords that are easily guessed and also OPT would be beneficial for text code via phone or email for additional authentication.
